#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9818ae35-c8b3-5f88-ba2b-5d45142a8bf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23+9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299254} {:answer/id #uuid "73906657-1425-51ce-a538-2aa005fbfb8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{36+9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299255} {:answer/id #uuid "47f2bfd2-8877-5c8d-baeb-95219fbfd2f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18+9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299256} {:answer/id #uuid "83b79d39-0888-504f-8c23-2da1887a9f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45+18\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299257} {:answer/id #uuid "87333285-38e3-5507-b35f-2d193c3ba5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87333285-38e3-5507-b35f-2d193c3ba5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299258} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1e47631-c32b-5c32-9d8a-459bc3b07a5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2db2de8c-b623-527a-a241-ca9def43988b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103771" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/68786f08e73998de597d0d9eea5be3031f322785c05835cf7eccfa7837f36d7b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. It consists of two lines and the arc of a semi-circle of radius $3.$ Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-3}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(6).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=6,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/b553755a624031408b8374d06c8dc8dbf33c3ddc8d372332c945bad7e42ef068.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [-3,0],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a triangle. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [0,6],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a semi-circle and a rectangle below it. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(6)={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [-3,0],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [0,6],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the semi-circle and the rectangle below it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & ={\\int }_{-3}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{9}{2}+(\\frac{9}{2}\\pi +18)=\\frac{45+9\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299259 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8a8d6e-233e-5b48-8976-c06358afb546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "73\\,225" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299261} {:answer/id #uuid "e39d5695-9efa-5e4a-ab6b-7ea5f9ffed29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69\\,270" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299262} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdb847b-1d92-5ccb-ba6b-56b98999ccef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89\\,850" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299263} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb86ed1-24f7-56df-a21e-7d7d67c11dde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\,250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299264} {:answer/id #uuid "3637a9d0-2e3a-5d25-a194-e01b7625e8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83\\,350" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3637a9d0-2e3a-5d25-a194-e01b7625e8e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "83\\,350" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299265} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ddf580e-bb8e-58b9-8619-130ffdcec2ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8de63944-9034-5911-a181-23fee9b4f403" :question/math-academy-id "q-103776" :question/problem "The bacteria culture in a lab increases at a rate of ${e}^{2t}$ per hour. If, after $2$ hours, there are $2\\,000$ bacteria in the population, approximately how many bacteria are in the population after $6$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "Let $s(t)={e}^{2t}$ be the rate at which the number of bacteria is changing and let $S(T)$ be the total number of bacteria and in the population after $T$ hours.\n\nUsing our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nS(T)=S(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(T) & =S(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{T}s(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\,000+{\\int }_{2}^{T}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}{|}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2T}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2T}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the number of bacteria in the culture after $T=6$ hours, we substitute in the above:\n\n$$\nS(6)=2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\cdot 6}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}=2\\,000+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{12}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4}≈83\\,350.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299266 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "639c3818-9c51-5dc5-abd5-c833fec45ac1" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Accumulation Function Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c6b9d63-00c5-5523-af96-e5386eabdd37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299268} {:answer/id #uuid "212160bb-b2d8-55d2-9748-1cb73911f520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299269} {:answer/id #uuid "d2bd244a-213a-55ab-9a18-cd8dada4f7ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299270} {:answer/id #uuid "4870f15d-97ab-552c-a447-e8dc2ef72fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299271} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ac04bc-2f57-5f8b-9435-10bae347d568" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4870f15d-97ab-552c-a447-e8dc2ef72fcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299271} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1e91b78-4ebe-5e83-b2a4-73b4a74119bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42f9929a-0a88-505d-9ee3-e11f565821f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-156194" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/1a3b8289936cd2597ac1252e2b28987f245a7c4e88dcf3677e1ba3626460688b.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(3).$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating $g(t)$ at $t=3,$ we get the following definite integral:\n\n$$\ng(3)={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral gives the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14038411/assets/b5becb438f3bc8ab5445e116ddb64bf168013301838231bdc4d32d34283da5f1.png)\n\nNote that the graph above consists of $2$ parts:\n\n- $x\\in [0,1],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is a trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{1}.$\n\n- $x\\in [1,3],$ for which the region between the curve and the $x$-axis is another trapezoid. This corresponds to the area ${A}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\ng(2)={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute these integrals:\n\n- To find the first integral where $x\\in [0,1],$ we compute the area ${A}_{1}$ of the trapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the second integral where $x\\in [1,3],$ we compute the area ${A}_{2}$ of the triapezoid as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2+3) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(2) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}+5=\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30eaa84d-9078-5589-bfbc-a6316643e872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180+3t+0.5t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30eaa84d-9078-5589-bfbc-a6316643e872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180+3t+0.5t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299275} :answer-field/id #uuid "db546a25-11bf-5aa0-aec7-adaf7e1a4438" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40532706-be5f-599b-966e-446d109da71f" :question/math-academy-id "q-316254" :question/problem "In $2005$, a group of scientists started tracking the population of sea turtles in a coastal sanctuary. The initial population was $180,$ and the birth and death rates are given by $4+1.5t$ and $1+0.5t,$ respectively, where $t$ is the number of years since $2005.$  \n\nFind an expression for the population $P(t)$ of sea turtles in the sanctuary at any time $t>0.$\n\n$P(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $p(t)$ be the rate of change of the population. According to the given information,\n\n$$\np(t)={{4+1.5t}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population increase}}-{{(1+0.5t)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{population decrease}}=t+3.\n$$\n\nLet $P(T)$ represent the population of turtles $T$ years after $2005.$ By the accumulation of change formula, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =180+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(t+3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =180+(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+3t){|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =180+\\frac{1}{2}{T}^{2}+3T.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable  $t.$ We get \n\n$$\nP(t)=180+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}+3t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a8be4bb-bbfe-5a5d-872c-91b4d67edc2f" :knowledge-point/title "Applications of Accumulation Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4306ae-9137-58e4-babb-e4d26499ac07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299278} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e238d5-6a1f-505d-a9f5-56554b7a8b1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}+2t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299279} {:answer/id #uuid "347de736-448e-59b9-82d1-caa49af24dfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299280} {:answer/id #uuid "223e8e98-6e9b-5be1-b669-6cafba50182b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299281} {:answer/id #uuid "289090e5-2d0e-5bc7-8f4c-aeb8567df58a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=6{t}^{2}-2t+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b4306ae-9137-58e4-babb-e4d26499ac07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299278} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c63586-7c82-5ed1-81b1-8e8127b1e4ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b01cc438-929e-58bd-85f1-0598039ce8e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-49315" :question/problem "A bicycle tire has a pressure of $6\\,\\text{psi}.$ It is being inflated, and the pressure is increasing at a rate of $4t\\,\\text{psi}$ per second, where $t$ is a time, in seconds, since the start of the inflation. The tire has a slow puncture that reduces pressure at a rate of $(t+2)\\,\\text{psi}$ per second. Identify an expression for $P(t),$ the total pressure in the tire at any time $t\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $p(t)=4t-(t+2)=3t-2$ be the rate at which the pressure in the tire is changing. Note that we subtract $(t+2)$ to indicate the rate at which the pressure is decreasing.\n\nLet $P(T)$ be the pressure in the tire after $T$ seconds. Then, using our formula for the accumulation of change, we have\n\n$$\nP(T)=P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known information and carrying out the necessary integration gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(T) & =P(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{T}p(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6+{\\int }_{0}^{T}(3t-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6+{(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t)|}_{0}^{T} \\\\  & =6+\\frac{3}{2}{T}^{2}-2T \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{T}^{2}-2T+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's rewrite our final answer in terms of the original variable $t.$ We have\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-2t+6.\n$$"}]}
